export const SEED_SKILLS = {
 "altitude": "---\nname: altitude\ndescription: Use when the user types /altitude, and when a task is one of a recurring stream (another bug, another speed fix, another chore): climb from the task to the outcome it serves and set up so that outcome is owned, not handed over task by task.\nargument-hint: \"[the task]\"\n---\n# Altitude: ask for the outcome, not the task\n\nA task is the user's best guess at a route. The outcome is what they want. Working at the outcome lets Claude pick a better route and stops the user from having to hand over task after task.\n\n## 1. Climb the ladder\nWrite three rungs, one line each, each a level higher:\n1. **Understand**: what is actually happening and why (the facts behind the task).\n2. **Decide**: the two or three most urgent choices it raises, each with for and against.\n3. **Redesign**: what the task is ultimately for, and a better way to get that than the current one, shown against past data (\"this would have caught last month's bug\").\n\nExample:\n- Task: \"fix this crash on the study board.\"\n- Rung 1: which crashes happen, on which devices, since which change.\n- Rung 2: which three to fix first; whether to add a crash log.\n- Rung 3: \"keep the study board crash-free\": a crash log, a repro per crash, a test that runs on every PR.\n\nPick the highest rung the user's goal and budget support, and say which in one line.\n\n## 2. Set up before owning it\nAn owned outcome needs, before the work:\n- the raw signal (logs, reports, failing inputs, feedback) and where it lives;\n- a way to reproduce and check each fix (tests, a script, a device or screen size);\n- a map from versions to changes (git history, PR list).\nList what's missing and build the cheap parts (a script, a test, a note) as part of the work.\n\n## 3. Make it last\n- Prefer a mechanism over a one-off fix: a test, a CI check, a script, a scheduled job, a handoff note, a shared skill.\n- It must run on free tiers and need no new setup from the user, or the setup is spelled out click by click.\n- Report what now runs without the user.\n\n## When not to climb\n- Creative or taste work (design, art, copy): stay at the task; use `range` or `two-ways`.\n- The user said exactly what and why: climb only to check the task serves that why. Mention a better route in one line; don't take it unasked.\n- Goals too high to act on (\"make money\"): climb only as far as a goal with a measurable check.\n- The tell that it's worth climbing: the same kind of task has come up three or more times.\n",
 "be-ambitious": "---\nname: be-ambitious\ndescription: Use when the user types /be-ambitious, and automatically at the start of a big or open-ended task: find the goal behind the task, pick altitude, depth or range, set an outside check, then work at that level.\nargument-hint: \"[the task]\"\n---\n# /be-ambitious\n\nTurn a task into its most useful version before starting. Three directions (from Felix Rieseberg's \"three types of ambition\", Oct 2026):\n- **Altitude** (up): work on the outcome, not the task. Skill `altitude`.\n- **Depth** (down): do it the way the best expert would, from raw material with their tools. Skill `depth`.\n- **Range** (sideways): bring in other fields. Skill `range`.\nTwo habits hold them up: skill `outside-judge` (never grade your own work) and skill `keep-going` (don't stop at \"impossible\").\n\n## Steps\n1. **Goal behind the goal.** One line: what should be true once this is done, and why the user wants it. Ask \"and then what?\" until the answer stops changing (usually one or two hops). Example: \"make the engine faster\" → \"a phone user gets a useful read on the study board within a second\".\n2. **Pick one or two directions**, rarely all three:\n   - Altitude: the user is after an outcome that keeps coming back (bugs, speed, quality, a recurring chore).\n   - Depth: the work has a right answer that experts reach with better data, tools or methods (engine speed, strength, data analysis, a hard bug).\n   - Range: the work is open (a feature, a page, a launch, an explanation people must understand).\n   Say in one line which and why the others were skipped.\n3. **Plan at that level** with the chosen skill: altitude's ladder, depth's four questions, range's verbs and crew.\n4. **Inputs.** List the data, tools and access the plan needs; mark what is here and what is missing. Get what you can yourself (read the repo, search, run a script). Ask for the rest once, batched.\n5. **Outside check.** Name the test, measurement or reviewer that decides pass or fail, and the pass line, before any work (`outside-judge`).\n6. **Brief.** Rewrite the task in at most 8 lines: goal, direction, plan in 3 to 6 steps, check and pass line.\n7. **Do the work** at that level, unless the user only asked for the rewritten prompt. Before a big or risky plan, run `premortem` on it.\n\n## Limits (ambition, paced)\n- The user's rules still hold: free tiers, budgets, one task per thread, ask before anything that costs money, deletes data or changes production. Ambition changes the method, never these.\n- A bigger goal than asked is a proposal, not a silent scope change. If it changes what gets delivered, say so in one line, deliver the asked task first, and take the bigger one only when it is cheap and clearly wanted.\n- Altitude suits outcomes, less so taste and creative work. Range costs tokens: the full crew only for important work.\n- Every \"better\" comes with a number from the outside check, including when it didn't help.\n- When this runs automatically, steps 1 to 6 take a few lines in the plan, not an essay. Skip it for small, clear tasks.\n",
 "clarify": "---\nname: clarify\ndescription: Use when the user types /clarify: ask 5 to 8 short questions as tappable choices in one batch before any work, so the job needs fewer turns.\n---\n# Clarify before work\n\nGoal: one question batch, one set of answers, then the work. Every extra turn costs tokens.\n\n## 1. Check what is already known (do not ask these)\n- The user's message and this chat.\n- If a repo is checked out: its `CLAUDE.md` and the one handoff note the task touches (Grep if unsure).\n- The files the task probably touches: Grep or Glob, then read only the lines needed.\nAnything answered there is not a question.\n\n## 2. Ask in one batch, as tappable choices\n- Ask at least 5 and at most 8 questions. Never type them as a plain list when a choice tool exists.\n- Pick the tool in this order:\n  1. **AskUserQuestion** (Claude Code CLI, desktop, web). If it is deferred, load it first with ToolSearch `select:AskUserQuestion`. It takes at most 4 questions per call, so for 5 to 8 send two calls in the same message (for example 4 and 3). Each question gets a `header` of 12 characters or fewer. Other is added automatically.\n  2. **ask_decision** cards (project threads, where AskUserQuestion does not exist): one card per question, all posted in the same turn, `recommended` set to the default, a `reason` for it. Labels 40 bytes max, consequences one sentence. Do not also repeat the questions in a reply; end the turn with a one-line reply or no_reply_needed.\n  3. Only if neither exists: one message, numbered questions with lettered options so the user can answer \"1a 2b\".\n- Each question: one line ending in \"?\", 2 to 4 options, the default first marked \"(Recommended)\" (on cards: `recommended`), a short description per option.\n- Pick the areas that fit, at least five: outcome; scope in; scope out; input data or example; constraints (cost, speed, free tiers, no new config); output (PR, file, reply, length); done check (tests or measurements); permission for anything that costs money, deletes data or changes production.\n- No task text given: the first question asks for the task.\n- The user may want 5 questions even when the task is clear: then check scope, output and tests, with Recommended options good enough to accept as they are.\n\n## 3. Act on the answers\n- Reply in at most 5 lines: \"Doing: ... Defaults used: ...\" then start. Use the Recommended option for anything skipped.\n- Ask again only if the work cannot continue, and then one question only, with the same tool.\n- Money, deletions or production changes wait for a clear yes.\n\n## Rules\n- Never ask what the user already answered or what the repo already says.\n- No preamble and no restating the task before the questions.\n",
 "depth": "---\nname: depth\ndescription: Use when the user types /depth or /deeper, and automatically when work has a right answer experts reach with better data, tools or methods (speed, strength, data, a hard bug): use raw material, real expert tools, a hard target and an outside check.\nargument-hint: \"[the task or prompt]\"\n---\n# Depth: do it the way the best expert would\n\nSounding like an expert is not depth; changing the method is. Ask what the best person in the field would actually do, from what raw material, with which tools, and how they would prove it.\n\n## 1. The four questions (before starting)\nAnswer each in a line or two, specific to this task:\n1. What would a top professional do here that the user didn't ask for?\n2. What is the rawest material they would start from instead of a summary? (logs, traces, the SGF files, KataGo's raw output, the real dataset, the source)\n3. Which methods and tools would they use? (profiler, benchmark harness, statistics with confidence intervals, a database, git bisect, a minimal repro, a held-out test set)\n4. What, other than Claude or the user, will show the result is right? (`outside-judge`)\n\nThen restate the task as the deeper version in 2 to 4 lines and do that. If the user sent `/deeper <prompt>` and only wants the rewrite, stop there. Deeper, not bigger: same scope, better method.\n\n## 2. Tools, not vocabulary\n- About to eyeball it or make a quick table in chat? Stop and ask: is this how the best person would do it? If not, use their tool (a script with real statistics, a profiler trace, a proper test).\n- Claude usually knows what experts use; the user doesn't need to.\n\n## 3. Unreasonable targets\n- Aim well past the obvious. If 2x looks easy, aim for 10x and find out what blocks it.\n- A target that looks impossible is hard, not impossible: switch to `keep-going` before giving up.\n- The target sets direction; the report says honestly what was reached.\n\n## 4. Go past what is known\n- **Challenge a belief**: take something the user or the docs assume and try to reproduce it from raw data. Report whether it held.\n- **Make new knowledge**: model the system from its raw data, find a signal nobody knew about, and show it predicts on data it wasn't built from.\n\n## Rules that still hold\n- Speed or strength claims: before and after with the project's own harness (in Dop: `scripts/ai-eval.ts`, Settings › Benchmark), same settings, enough runs to see the noise.\n- In Dop the LLM explains KataGo's numbers; depth never means the LLM inventing analysis.\n- Deeper methods that cost money or many LLM calls need a yes first.\n",
 "design-critic-panel": "---\nname: design-critic-panel\ndescription: Use when judging or iterating a visual design (landing page, app screen, board, logo) with critic agents, so scores are stable, comparable across rounds and lead to concrete fixes.\n---\n# Design critic panel\n\nWhy: past Kiai rounds used 5-10 Haiku critics scoring 0-10 from scratch. Scores sat at 4.5-7.7, swung about ±1 between rounds, and critics flipped advice (one round asked for X, the next for the opposite). This setup fixes the causes: no anchors, no memory, absolute scores, too-weak judges.\n\n## 1. Inputs, frozen for the whole run\n- **Rubric**: the user's own lines (e.g. XIAO's 8 rubric lines) verbatim, each with what a 3, 6 and 9 look like. Never reword it between rounds.\n- **Anchors**: 2-3 reference images the user likes (a 9) and one they called bad (a 3). Every critic sees them every round.\n- **Decisions log**: things the user already decided (e.g. \"no gradients, no glow, no pulses\", \"text at least 17 px\", \"no small grey text under titles\"). Critics may not reopen them.\n- **Stills**: the same set every round: desktop 1440x900 and phone 390x844, same scroll points, same theme, from headless Chromium. Add a crop at 2x for detail work (stones, wood, logo).\n\n## 2. Critics\n- Use a strong vision model for judging (Sonnet or Opus class); keep Haiku for cheap pre-checks only.\n- 5 critics, each owning 1-2 rubric lines (not everything), plus one generalist.\n- Each critic gets: rubric, anchors, decisions log, this round's stills, last round's stills, and last round's accepted fixes.\n\n## 3. Scoring: pairwise first, absolute second\n- Main question: \"Is this round better, same or worse than last round on your lines, and why?\" Pairwise judgments are far less noisy than 0-10.\n- Then a 0-10 per line, anchored to the reference images, with one sentence of evidence pointing at a region of a still.\n- Output strict JSON: `{line, verdict: better|same|worse, score, evidence, fix: {where, what, how_to_check}}`. At most 3 fixes, ranked.\n\n## 4. Aggregate\n- Median per line, not mean. Report the spread; if critics disagree by more than 2, show both views instead of averaging.\n- A fix is accepted only when 2+ critics ask for it, or one critic plus a measurable check (pixel colour vs reference, contrast ratio, font size, overlap test).\n- Measure what can be measured instead of asking a critic (e.g. sampled board colour rgb vs reference, text sizes, WCAG contrast).\n\n## 5. Loop and stop\n- Apply only accepted fixes, one round at a time, re-render the same stills.\n- Keep a round table: round, median per line, accepted fixes, what changed.\n- Stop when the gate is met, after 2 rounds with no median gain, or at the user's round limit. Never silently keep going past it.\n\n## 6. Report to the user\n- Before/after stills side by side, the round table, what is still open, and honest numbers (say when gains are within noise).\n",
 "design-shots": "---\nname: design-shots\ndescription: Use when checking how a web design looks: take a fixed set of desktop and phone screenshots and measure them against a reference image before any critic or user sees them.\n---\n# Design shots\n\nSame frames every round, and numbers instead of opinions where numbers exist.\n\n## 1. Capture\n- Headless Chromium via Playwright (in cloud sessions it is preinstalled; never run `playwright install`).\n- Fixed set, saved in one folder per round (`shots/round-N/`):\n  - desktop 1440x900 and 1280x800, phone 390x844 (deviceScaleFactor 2);\n  - the top of each page, each scroll chapter or screen the change touches, and any open menu or popup;\n  - a 2x crop of the detail being worked on (stones, wood, logo, text block).\n- Wait for fonts, images and WebGL to settle (wait for network idle plus about 1.5 s; freeze animations or pick fixed timestamps for motion).\n- Same theme, same data, same zoom every round. Name files `<page>-<viewport>-<step>.png`.\n\n## 2. Measure against the reference\n- Colour: sample the same regions in the shot and the reference (median rgb over a patch) and report the difference.\n- Layout: check text sizes (computed font-size), anything that overlaps or is cut off (element boxes vs the viewport), and WCAG contrast for text.\n- Proportions: main visual share of the screen (for example board height %).\n- Put results in a small table: check, target, measured, pass or fail.\n\n## 3. Hand off\n- Show before/after pairs side by side (previous round vs this one, same frame).\n- Give critics (see design-critic-panel) the shots plus the measurement table, so they judge taste and not things code can measure.\n- If `/taste` rules exist, run them on the shots first.\n- Report honestly: what passed, what failed, what was not checked.\n",
 "dop-dev": "---\nname: dop-dev\ndescription: Use for any work on the Dop / Kiai Go training site (KataGo, Doppelgänger, Kami/Kiai, coach LLM) to follow its hard rules, keep the session lean, verify and ship a ready PR.\n---\n# Dop development\n\nRepo: https://github.com/PERILTHESKYWING/Dop (owner XIAO). React 19 + TypeScript + Vite, zustand, IndexedDB, Web Workers, KataGo compiled to WASM (`engine/`), Vercel serverless `api/`. If the repo is checked out, its `CLAUDE.md` and `.claude/skills/` win over this file where they differ.\n\n## Hard rules (always)\n- LLM is Gemini Flash on the free tier: at most one call per user turn unless truly needed; reuse cached answers; short prompts with only the numbers needed.\n- Keys stay server-side (`LLM_API_KEY`, `LLM_MODEL`, `LLM_PROVIDER`, `OPENAI_API_KEY`): never `VITE_`, never in source, never committed, never asked for in chat. The app must work with no LLM.\n- The LLM explains KataGo's numbers; it never invents analysis, stones or walls. A built position is shown only after code checks and a final KataGo read.\n- ChatGPT/OpenAI is a training-only tone reviewer; the sites never call it.\n- Never commit network weights except `public/models/g170e-b10c128-*.bin.gz`. Keep `THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md` current.\n- Vercel Hobby (free); XIAO finds Vercel setup hard: avoid new config, spell out every click if one is unavoidable.\n- Any change under `public/engine`: bump `ENGINE_FILES` in `src/lib/engine/browserEngine.ts` (Vercel caches /engine for a week).\n- Speed or strength claims need measured before/after numbers (`scripts/ai-eval.ts`, Settings > Benchmark), same machine and settings. Report no gain or a regression honestly.\n- No force-push to main; never commit `.env` (only `.env.example`).\n- Ask first before anything that costs money, deletes data or changes production settings.\n\n## Lean session\n1. Read `CLAUDE.md` only. Never open `README.md` or `package-lock.json` whole. Open the one `docs/handoff/<topic>.md` that matches (ux, engine, llm, training, data, doppel, kami*, open-items) or Grep for a term.\n2. Find before reading: Grep or Glob, then Read with offset and limit.\n3. Keep weights, datasets, lockfiles and long logs out of context: summarise with wc, head, jq, grep; pipe long npm/vitest/tsc/vite output through `tail -40`; cap `git log` with `-n 30`; run `npm install --no-audit --no-fund --loglevel=error`.\n4. One task per thread; split extra asks and name which files each owns.\n\n## Engine or LLM change\n- Engine: read `docs/handoff/engine.md`, measure, bump ENGINE_FILES, log the numbers in that note in the same PR.\n- LLM: read `docs/handoff/llm.md`, keep or extend the code checker that rejects ungrounded answers, test the no-LLM fallback, update the note.\n\n## Verify (once, at the end)\n- While editing: `npx vitest run <path>` for the touched code only.\n- Before the PR: `npm run typecheck`, `npm test`, `npm run build`, each `2>&1 | tail -40`; report pass or fail per command.\n- Sweep the diff: ENGINE_FILES bumped if `public/engine` changed; no stray `.bin.gz`; `git grep -nE 'VITE_[A-Z_]*(KEY|SECRET|TOKEN)'` empty; no new Vercel config.\n\n## Ship\n1. Branch from fresh main, one topic per PR, small clear commits, `git push -u origin <branch>`.\n2. Open the PR **ready for review, never draft** (GitHub MCP `create_pull_request` with `draft: false`, or `gh api repos/PERILTHESKYWING/Dop/pulls ...` without `draft=true`).\n3. Body: Before, After, How, Checks. Plain words.\n4. Leave decisions and gotchas in the matching `docs/handoff/` note in the same PR.\n5. Tell XIAO in one line what to merge and any click they must do.\n\n## Replies\nShort, plain, answer first: what changed, check results, the one thing to do next.\n",
 "keep-going": "---\nname: keep-going\ndescription: Use automatically when about to call something impossible or too hard, or to stop at \"good enough\" short of the target: try the strongest untried routes, look for who has done it, ship a working version and improve it, then report honestly.\n---\n# Keep going\n\nMany limits are beliefs. People do better once they believe a goal is possible, see someone else reach it, and are allowed to fail on the way (the four-minute mile fell, then fell again 46 days later). Apply that to the work, and stay honest.\n\n## Before saying \"can't\" or \"this is the best possible\"\n1. Write what was tried and what blocked each attempt, one line each.\n2. Find who has done it, or something close: search for prior art, papers, open-source projects, earlier threads and handoff notes. Seeing it done changes what's plausible.\n3. List three untried routes that attack the blocker differently (another algorithm, doing less work, precomputing, caching, another tool, changing the question). Try the two most promising within the budget.\n4. Only then report, with numbers.\n\n## Ship, then improve\n- When the full target is far, ship a working version that is clearly better than before, and name the next step toward the target. Small, real and measured beats big and unfinished.\n- A failed attempt is a result. Record what was tried, the numbers and why it failed, so nobody repeats it.\n\n## Honest and paced\n- Keep going on method, never on claims: no rounded-up numbers, no \"done\" without the outside judge.\n- Respect the budget (`work-habits`). At the cap, stop and say where things stand and which route is next.\n- If it truly can't be done within the rules (free tiers, no invented analysis, no production changes without a yes), say so plainly and offer the closest thing that can.\n- Ambition needs a why: keep the user's goal in view, and pause when more effort no longer serves it.\n",
 "outside-judge": "---\nname: outside-judge\ndescription: Use automatically on any task whose result can be checked (code, speed, strength, a design, data, a claim): before starting, pick a check that isn't Claude's own opinion, set the pass line, run it, and report its numbers.\n---\n# Outside judge\n\nModels agree too easily, with the user and with themselves. Don't let the work grade itself: something else says pass or fail, ideally a tool that runs in seconds.\n\n## 1. Before the work\nName the judge and the pass line in one line. Prefer, in this order:\n1. A tool that runs now: tests, typecheck, build, a benchmark, a measuring script, a screenshot against a reference, a validator.\n2. Data the work wasn't built from: a held-out set, or history (\"would this have caught last month's bug?\").\n3. A reviewer that didn't see the work being made: a fresh subagent or another hub account, given only the goal and the result.\n4. The user, last, with one specific question (\"does the board feel faster on your phone? yes/no\").\nIf no judge exists, building a small one (a test, a measuring script) is part of the task.\n\n## 2. During\n- Run the judge after each meaningful change while it's cheap, not only at the end.\n- Never change the judge or the pass line to make a result pass. If the judge is wrong, say why and fix it openly.\n\n## 3. Report\n- Give the judge's own numbers: before and after, same settings, with the noise where it's measurable. Include results that didn't help.\n- Say \"not checked\" plainly for anything no judge covered. Never \"should work\".\n\n## Dop judges\n- Every PR: `npm run typecheck`, `npm test`, `npm run build` (`dop-verify`).\n- Speed or strength: `scripts/ai-eval.ts`, Settings › Benchmark.\n- Visual: `design-shots`, then `design-critic-panel`.\n- Coach text: checked against KataGo's numbers for the same position; any claim not in the data fails.\n",
 "premortem": "---\nname: premortem\ndescription: Use when the user types /premortem, and automatically before any big, risky or multi-round task: imagine it already failed, list the likeliest reasons, and change the plan to prevent them.\n---\n# /premortem\n\nAssume the task is finished and the user is unhappy with it. Work out why before any work starts.\n\n1. State the goal in one line, the way the user would judge it (what they will look at, and what \"good\" means to them).\n2. List 5 to 8 concrete ways it failed. Draw on:\n   - misreading the ask, or doing part of it and calling it done;\n   - the result looking fine in tests but wrong to the user (on a phone, in the real app, against their reference);\n   - unmeasured claims (speed, strength, quality);\n   - breaking something that already worked;\n   - cost (tokens, money, free-tier limits) or setup the user finds hard;\n   - past feedback from this user being repeated (check memory, /taste rules and handoff notes).\n3. Rate each one: likelihood (high, medium, low) and damage (high, medium, low).\n4. For every high item, add one prevention step to the plan: a check, a measurement, a screenshot, or a question to ask now.\n5. Output, short:\n   - Goal: one line.\n   - Top risks: a table of risk, likelihood, damage, prevention.\n   - Plan changes: the steps added.\n   - Question for the user: only if a high risk can't be prevented without their answer (at most one, with a recommended option).\n6. Then carry on with the work using the updated plan, unless the user only asked for the premortem.\n\nWhen it runs automatically, keep it to a few lines in the plan or status, not a separate essay. No generic risks that apply to every task.\n",
 "range": "---\nname: range\ndescription: Use when the user types /range or /crew, and on important open-ended work (a feature, a page, a launch, an explanation, a plan): turn the noun into verbs, bring in the fields that do them best, two of them critics, and use their methods, not role-play.\nargument-hint: \"[the task]\"\n---\n# Range: bring in other fields\n\nLook at the work through fields it never used to be worth consulting. This is where the options nobody thought of come from.\n\n## 1. Nouns to verbs\n- Write the noun the user asked for (\"a landing page\", \"a coach reply\", \"a review deck\").\n- Write what it actually has to do, as 2 to 4 verbs with their limits. Example: a coach explanation isn't \"a text\"; it has to make a 10-kyu player see why a move fails, remember it, and try it on the board, in under a minute.\n- Next to each verb, the field that does that verb best (teacher, game designer, editor, data scientist, sports coach, journalist, UX researcher, animator, ...).\n\n## 2. The crew: five fields, two of them critics\n- Three builders from step 1, each from a different field.\n- Two critics whose only job is to find what's wrong: a red-teamer (how does this fail or get abused), a skeptic who checks every claim against data, a forensic tester, a first-time user.\n- For each one, one line: what they would actually do, and their method (a lesson plan with spaced recall, a playtest, an edit pass that cuts 30%, a statistical check, a five-second test).\n\n## 3. Methods, not voices\n- No \"as a teacher, ...\" and no role-played conversations: they make the transcript stranger without improving the work.\n- Do what each method implies: build the playtest, run the stats, cut the copy, try the abuse case.\n- In the result, say in one line per field what it changed.\n\n## Cost\n- Default: run the crew inline, as one checklist in one reply. No subagents.\n- Separate agents only on important work and when the user agreed: the two critics are the ones worth separating, so the work isn't grading itself. For visual work the critics are `design-critic-panel`.\n- Tasks with one right answer (a typo, a speed fix) skip range; use `depth`.\n- The user's style rules still hold (in Dop: plain UI, short copy, free tiers). Range may suggest bigger ideas (a 3D walk-through, a video); offer them in one line with their cost, don't build them unasked.\n",
 "showtaste": "---\nname: showtaste\ndescription: Use only when the user types /showtaste: list XIAO's current design taste rules, numbered, from the taste skill.\n---\n# /showtaste\n\nShow the current rules of the `taste` skill. Change nothing.\n\n1. Get the newest saved version of the `taste` skill: if its text is already in this conversation, use it; otherwise load it with the Skill tool (`taste`, args `show`) or find its SKILL.md under the skills folders (for example Glob `**/taste/SKILL.md` in `~/.claude/skills`).\n2. Print only the `## Rules` list, numbered exactly as saved, with each rule's date.\n3. End with one line: \"/taste add <rule> to add, /taste remove <number> to remove.\"\n\nIf the taste skill can't be found, say it isn't saved to this account yet.\n",
 "taste": "---\nname: taste\ndescription: Use automatically whenever designing, building or reviewing anything visual (UI, page, landing, board, logo, image), and when the user types /taste to check a design or add or remove a rule.\n---\n# Taste\n\nXIAO's personal design rules. They come from real feedback on Dop, Kami and Kiai and override generic design advice.\n\n## Automatic use\n- Whenever you design, build or review anything visual, follow every rule below without being asked.\n- Before showing a design, check it against the rules, fix any break, and mention a rule only if you could not meet it.\n\n## Commands\n- `/taste` (no text): check the current design against every rule, list each break as `rule number: where it breaks: the fix`, fix what you can, and say which rules pass. Measure where possible (font sizes, colours, contrast, overlap) rather than guess.\n- `/taste add <rule>` (or `/taste <a new rule in plain words>`): add the rule to the end of the list in XIAO's own words, tightened to one line, with today's date. If it contradicts an existing rule, ask once which wins.\n- `/taste remove <number or words>`: remove that rule. Confirm which one if the match is unclear.\n- `/taste show`: same as /showtaste.\n\n## How an add or remove is saved\nThis skill's own file is the store. After an add or remove, propose an improvement of this `taste` skill (propose_skills, kind improvement, target `taste`) with the complete updated file: the list renumbered, nothing else changed. Then say in one line: \"Tap Save on the card to keep it.\" No proposal tool available: print the full updated list and say it must be saved in Settings to stick.\n\n## Rules\n1. Nothing should look cheap or \"AI generated\": one font family, flat pressable buttons, small corner radii, short copy. (2026-10-09)\n2. No decorative gradients. (2026-10-09)\n3. No glows, rings, pulses, ripples or light beams. (2026-10-20)\n4. No blue lights or neon accents. (2026-10-20)\n5. No small grey text under titles; text is at least 17 px on landing pages. (2026-10-20)\n6. No em-dashes in UI text. (2026-10-09)\n7. Nothing overlaps while scrolling, and nothing important is cut off at any screen size. (2026-10-20)\n8. Motion is calm: no fast cascades, every animation has a reason, holds let the eye rest. (2026-10-20)\n9. Every control does something visible; no decorative scroll bars or dead buttons. (2026-10-20)\n10. Match the reference image the user gave (colour, material, angle) and measure the match; \"close enough\" is not done. (2026-10-20)\n11. Go boards: real wood that matches the reference (honey kaya, not orange), stones with shading and shadows, and never an invented stone or wall in a diagram. (2026-10-20)\n12. Answers and key numbers are big and readable; the board or main visual gets the space. (2026-10-20)\n",
 "two-ways": "---\nname: two-ways\ndescription: Use when the user types /two-ways, and automatically when starting a new visual design or redesign: build two clearly different versions and let the user pick before polishing.\n---\n# /two-ways\n\nPick a direction first, polish second. Polishing one direction for many rounds wastes effort when the direction itself is wrong.\n\n1. From the ask, the reference images and the /taste rules, write two directions that differ in something the user will notice at a glance (layout, mood, material, density, motion), each in one line. Not two shades of the same idea.\n2. Build both to the same rough level: enough to judge the direction, not finished. Same content, same screen sizes.\n3. Screenshot both with the same frames (see design-shots if saved) and put them side by side, labelled A and B, with one line each on what it is going for and its main trade-off.\n4. Recommend one, with the reason, and ask the user to pick (A, B, or \"mix: X from A, Y from B\"). Use a decision card if the tool exists.\n5. Keep working on the recommended one while waiting; switch if the user picks the other.\n6. After the pick, delete the losing version's code so it doesn't linger.\n\nSkip it for small fixes to an existing design or when the user already chose a direction.\n",
 "work-habits": "---\nname: work-habits\ndescription: Use on every task, automatically and without being asked: check the result against the ask before saying done, recheck earlier fixes, respect budgets, batch non-urgent questions, and record lessons.\n---\n# Work habits (always on)\n\nFive habits for every task. They run without a command; the user can also call any one by name (\"done check\", \"regress\", \"lessons\", \"budget\", \"later\").\n\n## 1. Later: batch non-urgent questions\n- While working, don't stop for questions that have a reasonable default. Pick the default, note it, keep going.\n- Keep a list of these questions and defaults. Ask them all once, at the end, in one short numbered list with the default marked.\n- Stop and ask right away only for something that costs money, deletes data, changes production, or would make the whole result wrong.\n\n## 2. Budget: respect caps\n- If the user set a limit (rounds, time, cost, \"keep it cheap\", a score gate), write it at the top of the plan.\n- When the limit is reached, stop, say so in one line with where things stand, and ask whether to continue. Never drift past it.\n- No limit given on a long or open-ended job (design rounds, training, large searches): pick a sensible one, say it, and treat it the same way.\n\n## 3. Regress: recheck earlier fixes\n- Before shipping a new round of a task that had earlier rounds or feedback, list every issue fixed or rule set before (from the thread, handoff notes, /taste rules, memory).\n- Check each one still holds in the new result (re-run the test, re-take the screenshot, re-measure). Report any that came back and fix them first.\n\n## 4. Done check: before saying done\n- Re-read the user's original message (and later follow-ups). Split it into every separate ask.\n- Mark each: done (with evidence: link, file, number, screenshot), partly done (what's missing), or not done (why).\n- Anything partly or not done: finish it now, or say plainly that it isn't done. Never call a task done when an ask is missing.\n- Include the short checklist in the final reply only when something was missed or changed; otherwise just deliver.\n\n## 5. Lessons: at the end of a task\n- Write down 1 or 2 lessons as rules: what went wrong or what worked, and why, in a line each.\n- Save them where the next session will read them: project memory if there is one, else the repo's handoff notes in the same PR, else the end of the reply.\n- Skip it when there is nothing new to learn. Never save secrets.\n"
};
